<Docs title="How it works">
  <p class="draft">Draft outline</p>

  <h2>The problem</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Fading the edges of a field hints that there's more text, softer than a hard cut.</li>
    <li>On a text input, the browser scrolls the caret flush against the edge as you type, so it lands right under the fade.</li>
    <li>CSS has nothing to stop that: you can't tell an input to keep its caret clear of the edge.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What exists already</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Fading a scrolling container's edges is well known: Chrome's "soft edge content fade" guide, tw-fade, ngrok's scroll-fade.</li>
    <li>The CSS-only versions rely on scroll-driven animations, which Firefox hasn't shipped. In Zen (Firefox), tw-fade's blur and ngrok's fades don't appear.</li>
    <li>shadcn's <code>scroll-fade</code> is the most polished CSS-only version: a <code>mask-image</code> driven by scroll-driven animations, with logical start/end edges that mirror in right-to-left text. It's built for any scroll container (lists, cards, chat panes), not a text field. Without scroll-driven animations it falls back to a static fade on both edges. Check how that looks in Zen before publishing.</li>
    <li>CSS once proposed <code>text-overflow: fade</code> (2015 draft). Browsers never built it.</li>
    <li>None of them handle a caret.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Taking over the scroll</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The input grows to the exact width of its text and sits inside its own scrolling strip, so the strip, not the input, decides what's visible.</li>
    <li>Where is the caret? Measure the text before it with a hidden copy in the same font.</li>
    <li>Keep the caret at least <code>fade width − caret penetration</code> (22 − 4 = 18px) from a faded edge. At the very start or end, scroll fully, so nothing is faded where the caret is.</li>
    <li>Jumping to the start shows the prefix, but a long prefix never pushes the caret more than a third of the way across.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Fades that follow the scroll</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>First version: a 150ms fade-in when text became hidden. After a jump, the far edge showed unfaded text for 2–3 frames.</li>
    <li>Now each fade's strength is how much text is hidden on that side, reaching full strength at 22px. It changes in the same frame as the scroll, so there's nothing to catch up, and slow trackpad scrolling still looks smooth.</li>
    <li>The fade is eased, not linear: a straight ramp shows a visible band where it starts and ends. Eight stops per side follow a smoothstep curve.</li>
    <li>iOS selection handles: what was tried. Screenshots and demos of each still to make.
      <ul>
        <li>As is: iOS draws the handles inside the scrolling strip, so the strip clips them to the text line and the fade mask fades them.</li>
        <li>Room above and below the strip, cancelled with a negative margin: handles fit, but their shadows still clip at the ends, and taps up to 16px outside the field land in it. Only while focused: same, smaller.</li>
        <li>No scroll view (<code>overflow: hidden</code>): handles escape, but so does the selection highlight, past the field, and you can no longer swipe the field sideways.</li>
        <li>Chosen: the strip fills the field box (vertical padding on the element), so handles are clipped exactly at the box edge and taps stay inside it. It inherits the box's <code>border-radius</code>, so the clip follows its rounded corners.</li>
        <li>One leftover: iOS rounds the clip to whole pixels, so a 38.4px field leaves a sliver between the bottom handle and the focus ring. Confirmed against a 40px field, which is flush. Fix in the design: whole-pixel line heights.</li>
      </ul>
    </li>
    <li>It's a mask on the strip, so it works on any background with no colour to configure. Driven by JavaScript, so it works in Firefox too.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Arrow keys without taking them over</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The version on my site moved the caret itself (~120 lines): correcting after the browser moved it showed a one-frame jump.</li>
    <li>Taking over arrow keys means re-implementing emoji, right-to-left text, word jumps and Chinese/Japanese input.</li>
    <li>Sabi lets the browser move the caret, then corrects the scroll in the next animation frame, which runs before the screen is drawn.</li>
    <li>Browsers disagree on where a revealed caret lands: Safari centres it, Chrome stops short of the edge. Sabi corrects from the scroll position before the key press, so a word jump into hidden text stops at the edge everywhere. Found in hands-on QA, not by the tests.</li>
    <li>A caret scrolled into a fade with the trackpad stays drawn through the gradient and is hidden only in the fully faded edge, where the text is gone too.</li>
    <li>Evidence, from the tests in Chromium, Firefox and WebKit:
      <ul>
        <li>No correction at all: the caret is drawn under the fade.</li>
        <li>Correcting on <code>selectionchange</code> (the original approach): fails, the correction lands after paint, which is the original jump.</li>
        <li>Correcting in the next frame after the key press: passes in all three.</li>
        <li>CSS <code>scroll-padding</code> on the strip: also passes, because the browser's own caret scrolling respects it. Sabi uses both.</li>
      </ul>
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Right-to-left</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Approaches looked at before building: MUI's scrollLeft helper, textarea-caret-position's mirror, CodeMirror's bidi handling, shadcn's logical fade edges, the new <code>OpaqueRange</code> proposal.</li>
    <li>Scrolling: right-to-left <code>scrollLeft</code> starts at 0 and goes negative. Browsers used to disagree (MUI shipped a detector for three behaviours); every current engine now follows the spec (Chrome since 85). Sabi converts to "px hidden past the left edge", as MUI does, so the scroll and fade maths didn't change.</li>
    <li>The scroll range is rounded to whole pixels, and right-to-left Chrome can put it a pixel off where the text says (e.g. −402 to +1 instead of −403 to 0). Anything hidden within 1px counts as the edge.</li>
    <li>Finding the caret: the first version added up the width of the text before it. That breaks with mixed text (Hebrew with numbers or English), where a caret's place on screen stops following its place in the text. Now a hidden copy of the text is laid out like the input, and the browser says where the caret is, via a collapsed <code>Range</code>.</li>
    <li>Where runs meet, one caret position has two places on screen (end of one run, start of the other). CodeMirror tracks which one is drawn by running its own bidi algorithm, far too heavy here.</li>
    <li>First attempt: keep both places clear of the fades when they fit. Stepping into an English word then pulled the whole word into view, unlike anywhere in left-to-right text, and only in some browsers: a <code>Range</code> reports both places in Chromium, only the one by the character before in Firefox, only the other in WebKit.</li>
    <li>Second attempt: follow the place nearest where the caret just was. Right for single steps, wrong for Alt+arrow word jumps in Safari, where the caret vanished at the end of English words and numbers.</li>
    <li>Screenshots of a red caret, at every step, single and by word, settled it: Chromium and WebKit draw the caret beside the character running in the field's direction (the Hebrew side in a right-to-left field), the run with the lower bidi level. Sabi reads each character's box from the hidden copy: two characters that touch share the caret; where they don't, a run lies between, and which way each runs follows from where its own neighbour sits. No bidi algorithm needed. WebKit leaves a pixel between characters of a left-to-right run, so "touching" allows 2px.</li>
    <li>The text scrolls only as far as the caret moves, a character at a time, as in left-to-right text. Scroll corrections round toward keeping the caret clear, since WebKit scrolls by whole pixels.</li>
    <li>Arrow keys themselves differ: Safari and Firefox move by screen side, Chromium by text order, so in Edge the caret jumps to the far end of an English word and walks back through it. A plain input does the same, so it's left alone.</li>
    <li>Arrow keys still belong to the browser, which is why right-to-left needed no keyboard code at all.</li>
    <li>Fades were already per physical side, driven by how much is hidden on each, so they carried over. Only "start" and "end" (scroll fully at the ends, the one-third rule for long prefixes) swap sides. <code>data-fade-start</code> and <code>data-fade-end</code> name the fades by reading direction, as shadcn's logical edges do.</li>
    <li><code>dir="auto"</code> on the input: the field takes the input's direction, so a field flips as soon as someone types Hebrew or Arabic into it. Script sets a <code>data-dir</code> attribute that the stylesheet's layer turns into <code>direction</code>, so a <code>direction</code> set on the element itself still wins (an inline style, the first version, overwrote it). <code>:has(&gt; input[dir]:dir(rtl))</code> would do it in pure CSS, but needs Chrome 120, and where unsupported the field and its input would disagree on direction.</li>
    <li>An adversarial review by a second model (Codex) found the rest: a letter with an accent measured as two characters picked the wrong end of a word, a lone surrogate half threw, and typing mid-field in right-to-left text shifted it, because the scroll was restored in left-edge terms while the text grew on the left. Characters are now whole graphemes (<code>Intl.Segmenter</code>; Firefox before 125 falls back to code points, so only accents count apart there), and the scroll is restored as native <code>scrollLeft</code>. It also pointed out that the tests' caret probe mirrors the element's rule, so tests now also read the caret off screenshots, painted red.</li>
    <li>Later: <code>OpaqueRange</code> (Chromium, in origin trial) would read the caret straight from the real input, no copy needed.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Working with React</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><code>prefix</code> as an attribute crashed React 19: every DOM element already has a read-only <code>prefix</code> property, and React 19 sets custom-element props as properties. The prefix became the element before the input, matching the suffix after it.</li>
    <li>React could lose keystrokes (~1 in 5 Backspaces): Sabi updated its attributes mid-event, something watching them re-rendered React, and React wrote the old value back before seeing the edit. Sabi now waits for the next frame before touching the page.</li>
    <li>Importing on a server crashed (no <code>HTMLElement</code> there). Now safe.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How it's tested</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Playwright drives Chromium, Firefox and WebKit.</li>
    <li>To catch a one-frame flash, a probe reads the caret and fades at the very end of each frame, just before it's drawn.</li>
    <li>Every test is also run against deliberately broken code to prove it can fail. That caught tests that were passing without testing anything.</li>
  </ul>
</Docs>

